{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Delete Me for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The vacuum of space is a chaotic sea of \nquantum fluctuations. Some have said that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this vacuum energy can be harvested \nto build our future starship engines,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or manipulated to build warp drives. It \ncan't. But it is technically possible to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "move real energy through the quantum vacuum \nwithout it passing through intervening space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25600,
    "dDurationMs": 15360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum energy teleportation may be as close as we \nget to transporter beams. But how close is that?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone before we get started two quick \nannouncements. First we did our first Patreon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "live stream interview with Dr Matt Caplan about \nhow real black holes feast on their surroundings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and grow to such enormous sizes the live stream. \nIt will be made available through the community  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tab in the next week or so. We can't release \nit through the main feed because of algorithm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "related reasons so subscribe and hit the bell \nicon to be notified of community tab posts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next it's almost May the 4th be with you. It's \ntime for you to choose a side: you can selfishly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maintain your own thermal equilibrium at the cost \nof hastening the heat death of the universe with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our entropy strikes back t-shirt or sweatshirt \nor join the resistance signaling your opposition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to entropy with our smart thermos. But chaos is \nthe natural order so these items along with last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year's the force equals mass times acceleration \nwill only be available in May on the merch store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The laws of physics are pretty clear about a few \nthings. Energy can’t be created out of nothing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nor can energy or matter or even information \ntravel faster than light. The universe is very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106320,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strict about enforcing these rules, but it also \nseems pretty cool about us exploiting loopholes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, it turns out it's OK to draw energy \nfrom the vacuum of space as long as we put energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the vacuum somewhere else. And it’s also fine \nfor that energy to transfer between two distant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places without traveling the intervening space \nas long as something travels the same distance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in its stead. The protocol which makes this \npossible goes by the name Quantum Energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137440,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Teleportation, or QET for short, and scientists \nhave confirmed this bizarre effect in experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we get to that we have some \ntheory to learn. We have to learn about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum entanglement, quantum teleportation, \nand the quantum vacuum before we can do quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy teleportation. This’ll just take a \nfew minutes with some help from favourite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theoretical physicists, or should I say \nhypothetical physicists, Alice and Bob. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We start in Bob’s lab where he’s made a pair of \nentangled qubits, A and B, also known as a Bell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair. In this case the quantum information in \nthe qubits takes the form of the direction of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum spin—up or down. For this Bell pair, \nthe spin for each particle is undefined—it’s a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum superposition of both up and down. The \nonly thing that’s really known about the spins  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192400,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of A and B are that they are opposite. So Alice \nstops by to pick up qubit A and then goes back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to her lab and measures its spin. Let’s say she \nmeasures up. Because this is an entangled pair,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qubit B has no choice but to take on the value of \ndown. In other words, the wavefunction of the Bell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211600,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair collapses from up-down AND down-up to just \nup-down. Alice calls Bob and tells him that he has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a down qubit and he confirms with a measurement.\nThat’s a cute trick, but it would work equally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well if the spin of the particles were fixed from \nthe beginning. This gets more interesting if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice chooses different directions for \nher measurement—for example, measuring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left-right will ensure a left or right spin, and \nBob’s will still be opposite but on a different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "axis. This is a real superluminal or non-local \ninfluence, and in general this sort of experiment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is called a Bell test. We’ve covered it before.\nAlthough there are correlations between Alice’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choice of measurement axis and Bob’s readouts, \nit’s still not possible to send information  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster than light this way. Those correlations \ncan only be seen when Alice communicates to Bob  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "her measurement outputs or axis choices.\nStill, it’s weird—spookily weird according  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271920,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Einstein. There are ways to make entanglement \nweirder however. For example, Alice could take her  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half of the Bell pair and have it interact with \na new, independent qubit in a way that causes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob’s qubit to take on the quantum state of that \nnew qubit, meanwhile destroying the version held  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by Alice. It’s as though the quantum state \nof the new qubit is teleported. But again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob can only access this quantum state after \nreceiving classical information about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305120,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interaction from Alice. This is “regular” quantum \nteleportation and it’s been achieved multiple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times in experiments, with transfer of quantum \ninformation achieved over hundreds of kilometers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even between Earth’s surface and a satellite.\nMoving information around is nice, and actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "useful for things like quantum computers and the \nquantum internet. But what about moving actual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuff? We’re a long way off teleporting \nactual matter with transporter beams,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but matter is made of energy, so let’s start by \ntrying to move energy around. To do that we’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to expand the thought experiment a bit.\nThis time we’ll give Alice and Bob 10 Bell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs. If Alice measures the spin of all \n10, she’ll know the spin of all 10 of Bob’s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assuming he uses the same up-down measurement \ndirection. Let’s add one more feature.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll say the qubits are all suspended in a \nmagnetic field so that the spin up state is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly lower energy than the spin down state.\nIn the process of measuring the spin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371200,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob needs to either add energy into the system \nfor the low energy spin-up states or extracts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same amount of energy for the high-energy \nspin down states. Because he doesn’t know which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be up and which down, if he measures \nall 10 he’ll end up with a net zero energy. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if Alice does her measurements first, and \nis able to tell Bob exactly which qubits are up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and which are down? Then Bob can safely measure \nonly the qubits that will allow him to extract  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy from the system. Alice will always have \nspent more energy making the measurements than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob can extract, so energy conservation \nisn’t broken. But it certainly does feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Alice teleported her spent energy to Bob.\nThis all sounds a bit impractical. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before they can teleport energy they need to \nphysically exchange prepared qubits. That’s where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum vacuum will finally come back into it.\nAs a reminder, the vacuum in quantum mechanics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is not the passive nothingness of classical \nphysics. Every point in space is permeated by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum fields, whose excitations give us the \nparticles and forces. The lowest energy state  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these fields is typically zero, which means \nno particles—a perfect vacuum. But due to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446960,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg uncertainty principle, quantum \nsystems can’t take on perfectly defined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450800,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "values. So the zero energy state is only \napproximately zero. It’s zero on average,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but when you make a measurement at a given spot \nit might be a bit larger or smaller than zero,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the fluctuations witnessed by an individual \nseries of measurements seem completely random. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try an analogy. Imagine a pond. There’s a \nvery light rain, so that at any time the surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the pond is a pattern of overlapping ripples. \nThe height of the ripple at any one point is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subtly correlated with the entire ripple pattern. \nYou add a device to track the ripple height at one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point. On its own, it seems to fluctuate \nrandomly. But measuring at two different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spots you’ll see correlations due to the fact that \nyou’re measuring correlated ripples. In principle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you could even extract energy from random-seeming \nup-down motion by using the knowledge you gain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the global ripple pattern. Not only that, \nbut your measurement itself causes ripples,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which influences the measurements \nyou make elsewhere on the pond. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum vacuum is a bit like the surface \nof our pond, but now all oscillations at all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequencies—both positive and negative—are \nsimultaneously present but balanced in such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534160,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a way that the pond—the vacuum—looks still or \nempty. But when you look close you can’t help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but interact with the vacuum, and suddenly \nthe wild fluctuations can become apparent.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just like the pond surface, fluctuations at \none point are correlated with all other points.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554160,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each point is entangled with every other point \nin the true quantum sense. So, while measurements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the quantum vacuum at any one point yield \nrandom energies that average to zero, those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same quantum fluctuations are correlated with the \nfluctuations measured at any other spot, even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spots are separated by large distances.\nSo the idea of quantum energy teleportation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that this quantum entanglement can be used \nto extract energy from one location based on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurements made at a completely different \nlocation. It’s the same idea as our energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extraction from particle spins, except this \ntime we don’t pre-prepare entangled pairs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instead we use the preexisting \nentanglement in the vacuum itself. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The process of extracting entanglement from the \nquantum vacuum is called entanglement harvesting.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an interesting topic all on its own—it \nsuggests that two measurement devices can become  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entangled just by measuring spatially separated \nbut entangled regions of the quantum vacuum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This entanglement should be able to be used as \na resource for teleportation-like protocols. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626320,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so in 2008, physicist Masahiro \nHotta proposed the protocol of Quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energy Teleportation. Here’s \na crude idea of how it works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say Bob now wants to extract free energy \nfrom the quantum vacuum. Because he’s actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studied physics, he knows that this is impossible \non its own. So, once again, Bob calls Alice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This time Alice doesn’t even have to travel \nto Bob to collect an entangled particle. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum vacuum at her location is \nalready entangled with the vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at Bob. She can make a measurement and \nsend the results of that measurement to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665920,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob by classical channels and Bob can use \nthat information to extract real energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at what the quantum fields are actually \ndoing in the QET protocol. As I mentioned earlier,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can think of the quantum vacuum as being \nmade up of every possible frequency mode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which together cancel each other out to yield the \napparent emptiness—zero average energy—observed on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large scales. But this balance can be disturbed. \nIt’s disturbed by black holes to produce positive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy outside the black hole and negative \nwithin—that’s Hawking radiation, and it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disturbed by the measurements made by Alice and \nBob. Alice’s measurement yields a positive energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that means she has injected energy into \nthe vacuum. She disturbed the vacuum so the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underlying vibrational modes no longer cancel, \nbut rather yield an excited state. Bob then uses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the information from Alice’s measur 1ement \nto tune his own measurement, which ensures  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726960,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he sees the vibrational modes that were depleted \nto power Alice’s excitation. Bob sees a negative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735840,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy density in the vacuum, and finds that the \nmissing energy is now in his measuring device."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice needs to input at least as much energy \nas Bob extracts. That’s why this protocol feels  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like energy teleportation—energy injected \ninto the quantum vacuum at one location is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extracted at a different location without \nit ever taking the form of real particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn't violate energy conservation. It \nalso doesn't  violates relativity. Because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the measurement information Alice sends to \nBob still has to travel by classical channels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This idea of extracting energy from \na seemingly random, chaotic system by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using detailed information about the system \nis not new. It’s the same idea as Maxwell’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demon. In this thought experiment, we start \nwith a gas at a uniform temperature. Energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t be extracted from it because it’s already in \nequilibrium. But we introduce an agent—Maxwell’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demon—who carefully monitors the trajectories \nof particles and sorts the particles according  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to temperature. This produces a temperature \ngradient, so work can now be extracted from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gas. It seems like we got energy out of \nthin air, but really the demon had to expend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy to do the computations to sort the gas—and \nexpends at least as much energy as was extracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like Alice had to expend energy to \n“sort” the quantum vacuum for Bob. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference with quantum energy teleportation is \nit’s not possible to directly monitor the local  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum and then extract energy from it—that \nlocal activity puts more energy into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum than can be extracted. That’s what’s \ncool about quantum energy teleportation—it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841680,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a way to “Maxwell’s demon” the vacuum itself \nfrom a remote location so it can be harvested."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, is quantum energy teleportation even possible? \nThe answer seems to be yes, although not yet via  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the vacuum. It was first demonstrated in 2022 \nby a team of physicists primarily based out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Institute for Quantum Computing (IQC) in \nWaterloo Canada. They used entangled qubits in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869600,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the form of the quantum spin of carbon atoms in \na molecule of transcrotonic acid—more commonly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used to create paints and glue. The qubits were \nstabilized and manipulated using nuclear magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resonance—the same physical phenomena which \nallows MRI machines to work. A pair of entangled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qubits in their lowest energy states were used as \nproxies for the quantum vacuum and a third qubit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to mediate the measurement. The team found that \nthe correct measurement process enabled energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be deposited in one qubit and then extracted \nfrom its entangled partner despite the latter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being in the lowest energy state, so in principle \nhaving no energy to give. This energy transfer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "occurred much more quickly than it would normally\ntake energy to travel naturally in this system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This successful demonstration of quantum \nenergy teleportation was quickly followed up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by an independent experiment in 2023 using \nIBM’s superconducting quantum computer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confirming that QET seems to be \npossible in realistic lab settings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of energy transferred \nin these experiments is very small.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same will be true if we ever manage to do \nthis via vacuum entanglement. The latter will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also have a very limited range. But this \ncould still be useful in quantum computers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952240,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and various nanodevices. But beyond moving \nenergy around, QET experiments might be even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more valuable for understanding the nature \nof entanglement and the quantum vacuum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that QET in the quantum vacuum \nleads to a negative energy density.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the type of energy density required in \ngeneral relativity to produce exotic effects like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wormholes and warp drives. We’ve known for some \ntime that negative energy densities are possible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example in the Casimir effect where \ncertain vibrational modes are excluded from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the space between parallel conducting plates. \nBut the Casimir effect requires the presence of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these plates, which more than cancel out the \neffect of the negative energy between them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the cool thing about the QET negative energy \ndensity is that it can be generated remotely,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which in principle may mean that \nits effect on spacetime curvature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be studied. And almost certainly will \nprove to be useless for faster than light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion or the inevitable capacity for time \ntravel that results from FTL. The universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to be generous only with loopholes \nthat do NOT violate relativity this way.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But quantum energy teleportation could give \nus a very new understanding of the connection  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031920,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between negative energy, vacuum entanglement, \nand the curvature and even fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to DeleteMe for supporting PBS. DeleteMe \nwas created to help protect its members from data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brokers—companies that use automated tools to \nscrape public records, harvest social metadata,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and buy commercial datasets to build detailed, \ncross-linked profiles. These profiles are then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sold across industries, often without (their \nsubjects’) knowledge. DeleteMe was built to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "help protect your personal data and help you \nprevent things like doxxing, harassment, or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identity theft. Their system scans hundreds \nof broker sites, flags exposures, and sends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074640,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formal takedown requests. Ongoing monitoring is \nin place to help quickly handle any data that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reappears. With a family plan, DeleteMe extends \nthese protections to linked individuals—to help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimize household exposure by disrupting \nhow brokers infer relational connections.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can get 20% off any DeleteMe consumer or \nfamily plan at joindeleteme.com/spacetime with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "promo code spacetime. There’s \na link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supporting us on Patreon is one of the strongest \nways to support our channel and you’ll get access  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to our livestream interviews. Today \nwe're shouting out David Paryente,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who’s supporting us at the Big Bang level \non Patreon and chose to honor Lise Meitner  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123600,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with his shoutout. Meitner was instrumental \nin explaining nuclear fission—using Bohr’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid drop model to show how uranium could \nsplit and release vast amounts of energy. Her  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insight turned experimental results into \none of the most important discoveries of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1140640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "20th-century physics. It proved that E=mc² \nwasn’t just theory—it was an engine. David,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thank you for spotlighting her legacy and \nhelping us continue to tell the stories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1152080,
    "dDurationMs": 22092,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of physics' unsung heroes. We’re honored to \nhave your support in the Space Time continuum."
    } ]
  } ]
}
